  "status": "reviewed",
  "steps": {
    "hook": {
      "text": "The black keys have no letters of their own. Press the three glowing keys in turn: C, the black key in the middle, then D. What would you call that black key?",
      "exhibit": {
        "kind": "keyboard",
        "highlight": [
          "C4",
          "C#4",
          "D4"
        ],
        "low": "C4",
        "high": "C5"
      }
    },
    "explore": {
      "text": "Play A. Then play the very next key up. Now play B, and then the very next key down. Both times you land on the same key.",
      "instrument": "keyboard",
      "exhibit": {
        "kind": "keyboard",
        "highlight": [
          "A4",
          "B4"
        ],
        "low": "C4",
        "high": "C5"
      },
      "goal": "Play the black key between A and B once from each side."
    },
    "name": {
      "cards": [
        {
          "text": "A [[sharp|sharp]] ♯ raises a note to the very next key up. A [[flat|flat]] ♭ lowers it to the very next key down. The black key between A and B is A♯ and also B♭: one key, two names. Sharp goes up, flat goes down.",
          "exhibit": {
            "kind": "keyboard",
            "highlight": [
              "A4",
              "A#4",
              "B4"
            ],
            "low": "C4",
            "high": "C5"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": "A [[natural|natural]] ♮ cancels a sharp or flat and puts the note back to its plain self. On the [[stave|stave]] the sign goes before the note head. Sharps, flats and naturals are all called [[accidental|accidentals]].",
          "exhibit": {
